      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "<a href=\"/movie/100205\" class=\"movie-title\">Baby Snakes</a> (1979) captures Frank Zappa at his most theatrically subversive, combining live concert footage from his 1977 Halloween show at New York's Palladium with surreal clay animation by Bruce Bickford. The film's anarchic structure mirrors Zappa's compositional style - it's deliberately challenging, frequently absurd, and fiercely uncompromising. Like other maverick music documentaries like <a href=\"/movie/24128\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"24128\">Stop Making Sense</a> (1984) and <a href=\"/movie/13963\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"13963\">The Last Waltz</a> (1978), it transcends simple concert coverage to become a genuine artistic statement."
      },
      {
